/* ClaimTract main article: V1-V4 full versions with a voice switcher. Used by the sample pages and the build preview. */
(function(){
var V={"1": {"title": "Public Adjuster vs. Insurance Adjuster: Who Works for You on a Homeowners Insurance Claim, and Who Doesn't?", "lead": "<p>The inspector has been at your house. He looked at the stained ceiling, took a few pictures, and left.<\/p><p>But the water reached the bedroom next door. Nobody looked inside that room. Nobody measured the damaged floor.<\/p><p>A few days later, an estimate arrives. The room you were worried about is not listed.<\/p><p>Who decides what makes it into that estimate? Is it the person who visited your home, someone reviewing the file at a desk, or someone higher up?<\/p><p>That is where many homeowners get confused. The person you meet may not be the person reviewing the paperwork. And neither person works for you simply because they are handling your claim.<\/p><p>Understanding who does what can help you ask better questions, keep better records, and know when you may need someone representing your side.<\/p>", "secs": [{"h": "The moment your claim gets a number", "html": "<p>When you report damage, the insurance company opens a claim and gives it a claim number. That number becomes the reference for the paperwork, calls, inspection, and later decisions.<\/p><p>The company may assign several people to handle different parts of the file. You might meet one person at your home and never speak directly with the others.<\/p><p>The important distinction is who each person works for. A company staff adjuster is employed by the insurance company. An independent adjuster is hired by the company on a contract basis. Both work for the insurance company.<\/p><p>A public adjuster is different. You hire that licensed insurance professional to represent you, the policyholder. A public adjuster documents damage, reads your policy, prepares the claim, and negotiates with the insurance company on your behalf.<\/p><p>Those are different responsibilities. A person inspecting your home for the insurance company is not your personal representative. You can ask anyone handling the claim who they work for.<\/p><p>In Florida, public adjusters must hold a state license. You can check a license through the Florida Department of Financial Services.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, what is the first question you wish homeowners would ask when someone contacts them about an insurance claim?"}, {"h": "The field adjuster: the person who visits your home", "html": "<p>The field adjuster is often the person you meet in person. This person visits the property, looks at the reported damage, takes photographs, and gathers information for the claim.<\/p><p>That visit can feel like the most important moment in the process. But the inspection is only one part of the claim. The written estimate and later review may involve people you never meet.<\/p><p>A field adjuster may be an independent contractor hired to inspect a property. That person may work with several insurance companies. The field adjuster is usually gathering information, not making the final payment decision.<\/p><p>Pay attention to what gets inspected. Did the person look at every affected room? Were the floors, walls, ceilings, and damaged belongings included? Did the inspector take photographs of the areas you pointed out?<\/p><p>Sometimes the inspection focuses on the area first reported. A roof inspection, for example, may not automatically include a room where water later appeared. Water damage can extend beyond the first visible stain.<\/p><p>You should explain what happened and point out every area you believe was affected. You can also ask whether those areas will be included in the inspection report.<\/p><p>Take your own photographs and video when it is safe. Start with the full room, then photograph the details. Keep damaged materials when practical, or photograph them before they are removed.<\/p><p>If you discover damage after the inspection, document it. You can ask the insurance company how to submit the additional photographs and information or request another inspection.<\/p><p>The key is to compare what the inspector saw with what actually happened in your home. A visit does not help much if important damage never makes it into the record.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, describe an anonymous example of something important a homeowner should point out during a property inspection. What should the homeowner make sure gets documented?"}, {"h": "The desk adjuster: the person reviewing the paperwork", "html": "<p>You may never meet the desk adjuster. This person usually works from the claim file rather than visiting your property.<\/p><p>The desk adjuster reviews the claim notes and inspection information. The desk adjuster may also give the field adjuster instructions about what to inspect before the visit.<\/p><p>That matters because the first report of damage can shape what gets looked at. If the original report mentions one room or one part of the roof, the inspection may focus there. Damage outside that area can be missed unless someone brings it forward.<\/p><p>After the inspection, the desk adjuster commonly reviews the report and estimate. That review helps determine what damage and repair work appear in the insurance company's evaluation.<\/p><p>You should not assume that every item you mentioned during the inspection appears in the final estimate. Read the estimate and compare it with your own photographs and records.<\/p><p>Look at the rooms and areas listed. Check the repair work described. Compare the measurements and damaged materials with what you can see in your home.<\/p><p>If something is missing, ask for an explanation. Send photographs, contractor findings, receipts, or other records that help document the issue. Keep a copy of what you send and note when you sent it.<\/p><p>The desk adjuster may be working with information gathered by someone else. Your job is to make sure the file reflects the damage you are reporting. A clear written record gives the reviewer something specific to consider.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, what is the first thing you look for when you compare an insurance estimate with the damage documented at a property?"}, {"h": "The supervisor: when the file moves higher", "html": "<p>A desk adjuster may have limits on the amount of authority available for a claim. When a claim involves a larger amount or a decision outside that adjuster's authority, a supervisor may become involved.<\/p><p>The supervisor is a manager who may review the claim and the decisions made below that level. Supervisors also watch claim figures and the amounts being paid across the work they oversee.<\/p><p>This can affect what happens when you disagree with an estimate. The desk adjuster may not have authority to approve every requested amount. The file may need another level of review.<\/p><p>That does not mean a supervisor will automatically change the estimate. It means the homeowner should understand whether the issue has been reviewed and what explanation supports the decision.<\/p><p>A detailed estimate can make that conversation more useful. It should identify the damaged areas, the work needed, and the amounts being requested. Photographs and other records can help support those details.<\/p><p>If you are told the claim is waiting for approval, ask what is being reviewed. Ask whether the reviewer needs more information. If a decision has been made, request the explanation in writing.<\/p><p>You do not need to guess how much authority someone has. Ask whether the person reviewing the file can address the issue you raised. Keep the answer with your claim documents.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, when a homeowner says a claim has been sent to a supervisor, what should that homeowner ask next? What can the homeowner do while waiting?"}, {"h": "The legal department: when the disagreement becomes a legal matter", "html": "<p>The legal department is another layer a homeowner may hear about, especially when a claim becomes disputed.<\/p><p>Most claims do not begin with a lawyer handling the homeowner's everyday questions. The field adjuster inspects. The desk adjuster reviews the file. A supervisor may review decisions that need another level of approval.<\/p><p>Legal staff may become involved when a dispute develops into a legal issue. Insurance attorneys handle legal disputes and lawsuits. Their role is different from the person who inspects the property or reviews the estimate.<\/p><p>A legal referral does not, by itself, tell you what the final outcome will be. You need to read the actual letter or communication and understand what it says.<\/p><p>If you receive a denial or a letter explaining the insurer's position, keep the entire document. Look for the reason given and any policy language identified. Ask for an explanation in writing if you do not understand the decision.<\/p><p>A reservation-of-rights letter is one document worth understanding. It means the insurance company is still reviewing coverage. It is not the same as a denial, but it deserves attention.<\/p><p>You should not ignore a legal or policy letter because the wording is difficult. Keep it with your claim records and get help reviewing what it says. If you need legal advice, speak with a qualified attorney.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, what should a homeowner do when an insurance letter uses language that sounds legal or threatening? What should the homeowner bring to a review?"}, {"h": "What you can do at every stage", "html": "<p>You cannot control how the insurance company assigns its employees or contractors. But you can control how carefully you document your own claim.<\/p><p>Start with the damage. Take photographs and video before cleanup when it is safe. Keep receipts for emergency work and save copies of estimates and reports.<\/p><p>A short inspection can leave questions. But your own photographs and records can help show what was there and what changed.<\/p><p>Keep every letter, email, estimate, and receipt together. Write down the claim number and the dates of important calls. Note who you spoke with and what was discussed.<\/p><p>When an estimate arrives, compare it with the damage you documented. If a room or item is missing, ask why. If the answer is unclear, request it in writing.<\/p><p>A denial or low estimate does not mean you should stop asking questions. You can ask for the reason, review the policy language, and provide additional documentation.<\/p><p>There may be another inspection or another level of review. A public adjuster can review the policy, document the damage, prepare the claim, and negotiate with the insurance company on your behalf.<\/p>", "q": ""}, {"h": "Who works for you?", "html": "<p>The people handling your insurance claim may have different titles and different jobs. The field adjuster may visit your home. The desk adjuster may review the report. A supervisor may review decisions or amounts beyond the desk adjuster's authority. Legal staff may handle disputes and lawsuits.<\/p><p>Those people work on the insurance company's side of the claim. A public adjuster is hired by you and represents your interests as the policyholder.<\/p><p>Before signing an agreement, check the public adjuster's license. Read the written fee agreement and ask the adjuster to explain the charges. Do not sign because someone is rushing you.<\/p><p>You deserve to understand who is handling your claim. You also deserve to know who is responsible for presenting your side.<\/p><p>When you look at the people who have handled your claim so far, do you know who each one works for, what they have documented, and who is responsible for representing your side?<\/p>", "q": ""}]}, "2": {"title": "Public Adjuster vs. Insurance Adjuster: Who Works for You on a Homeowners Insurance Claim, and Who Doesn't", "lead": "<p>A homeowners insurance company puts four layers of people on your claim file—a field adjuster, a desk adjuster, a supervisor, and a legal department—and every one of them works for the carrier, not you. Under Florida law, you have the right to hire a state-licensed public adjuster who inspects the entire loss, reads your policy, and negotiates strictly on your behalf.<\/p>", "secs": [{"h": "The Envelope on the Kitchen Table", "html": "<p>A homeowner opens an envelope from the insurance company and finds a check for half of what a local contractor quoted. The repair sheet inside lists a single room or a small patch on the roof, while water stains spread across the ceiling next door. Yet the letter reads like a final decision, leaving the family wondering how one short visit produced such a small number. Seeing who sits behind that paperwork is the first step toward protecting a claim.<\/p><p>When damage hits a home—from a storm, a burst pipe, a fire, or a sudden leak—the insurance company opens a file right away. Most homeowners assume the person who knocks on the door with a measuring tape is there to make sure the family gets paid fairly. Still, every person the carrier puts on that file works for the insurance company, not the policyholder. That gap between expectation and reality shows up on kitchen tables across Florida every week, and knowing each role on the company's side makes it easier to spot what was missed.<\/p><p>Under Florida law, a homeowner does not have to walk through that process alone. You have the right to bring in your own licensed representative, known as a public adjuster, to handle the claim on your behalf. Before signing any paperwork or treating a low first payout as the end of the road, it helps to see how the two sides of a claim are built.<\/p>", "q": "Think about a homeowner who called you after getting a first check or repair sheet that shocked them—what was the gap between what the insurance company listed and what the house actually needed, without naming the client or address?"}, {"h": "The Moment a Claim Number Is Issued", "html": "<p>Calling in a loss feels like a simple paperwork step at first. You report what happened, write down your claim number, and wait for someone to come look at the house. However, that single phone call sets four separate layers of people in motion inside the insurance company.<\/p><p>You will likely only ever meet one of those four people in person. The other three work behind the scenes from offices and departments you never see. Because they never walk your property, those three layers rely entirely on the notes, photos, and measurements put into your file.<\/p><p>A single missing detail in that initial report can shape how every later desk handles the loss. Each layer has a specific job in reviewing, pricing, and approving your claim. Once you see what each role actually does, the delays and gaps in a repair estimate start to make much more sense. You can then focus on putting clear, documented facts into the file where every layer has to read them.<\/p>", "q": "When a homeowner first calls you right after reporting a claim, what is the single most common assumption they have about how the insurance company is handling their file?"}, {"h": "Layer One: The Field Adjuster", "html": "<p>The field adjuster is the person who shows up at your front door with a camera, a ladder, or a moisture meter. Sometimes this person is a company staff adjuster employed directly by the insurance company. Often, though, they are an independent adjuster hired on a contract basis who may handle claims for several different insurance companies at once.<\/p><p>Whether staff or independent, the field adjuster works for the insurance company, not for you. They are usually friendly and polite while measuring and taking photos of the area they were told to inspect. Yet in many cases, the field adjuster does not have the authority to decide what your claim pays.<\/p><p>Their job is to gather information and send a report back to the office. During a quick ten-minute walkthrough focused on one roof slope or a single stained ceiling, wet insulation, swollen baseboards, and attic damage easily go unrecorded. Taking your own wide and close-up photos before cleanup—and saving damaged parts like a cut pipe or torn drywall—gives you proof even when that first visit is brief.<\/p>", "q": "What is one specific area or detail inside or outside a Florida home that you routinely find field adjusters skipped during a quick initial walkthrough?"}, {"h": "Layer Two: The Desk Adjuster", "html": "<p>The desk adjuster is the person handling your claim file from an office you never visit. They read your initial claim notes first and frequently tell the field adjuster which specific area to inspect. If your first phone call only mentioned the roof or one wet room, the inspection may be set up for that single spot alone.<\/p><p>Because the desk adjuster never steps foot in your house, they only know what is written in the file. They review the field adjuster's photos, apply your policy rules and deductible—including separate Florida hurricane deductibles of 2%, 5% or 10% of the home's insured value—and build or approve the repair estimate. Still, an incomplete report does not close the door on recovering the rest of your loss.<\/p><p>A desk file moves when concrete proof replaces guesswork. Submitting clean room-by-room photos, moisture checks, and local repair pricing gives the office adjuster a complete record to evaluate. Every letter, estimate, and photo set you send becomes part of the permanent file that desk adjuster must review.<\/p>", "q": "When you send a supplemental estimate or new photos to a desk adjuster, what piece of documentation tends to get the clearest response on a stalled file?"}, {"h": "Layer Three: The Supervisor", "html": "<p>Above the desk adjuster sits a supervisor or manager who oversees claim files and payout limits. A desk adjuster's authority can stop at a set dollar limit, meaning larger repairs or disputed items have to go higher up for review. Even when a field adjuster sees major damage, the final check may wait on sign-off from that next level.<\/p><p>Homeowners rarely speak directly with the supervisor reviewing their file. Your claim can sit waiting in review while a manager checks total payout numbers, repair scope, and policy limits. When a file comes back from that level, homeowners often see cuts to local labor rates, material quantities, or building code upgrades. Yet a supervisor reviews files based on policy language and documented proof, not phone calls asking for a favor.<\/p><p>Larger residential claims often stall at this stage because key construction steps were left out of the original notes. For example, an initial estimate might pay to patch one small section of a roof or paint a single wall without accounting for brittle older materials or matching rules across an open room. Supervisors look at whether the file proves why a full repair is required rather than a spot patch.<\/p><p>A documented, line-by-line repair estimate backed by clear photos and your policy's declarations page gives a supervisor concrete facts to review. Instead of arguing over opinions, the file shows exact measurements, local repair bids, and the exact endorsements or exclusions that apply. Having a licensed representative who knows how to organize that proof in the format a supervisor expects keeps the review focused on the facts of the loss.<\/p>", "q": "When a claim gets stuck above a desk adjuster's authority limit, what is the biggest mistake homeowners make when trying to get a supervisor to approve the full repair scope?"}, {"h": "Layer Four: The Legal Department", "html": "<p>In the background of every insurance company sits the legal department, along with outside insurance attorneys. As long as a homeowner accepts the first check and nobody questions a low estimate or a partial denial, lawyers never have to step into the file. When a dispute drags on, a claim grows complex, or a carrier issues a formal coverage denial, legal staff may review the letters and policy exclusions.<\/p><p>Many homeowners see a formal letter quoting dense policy sections—or a \"reservation of rights\" letter saying coverage is still under review—and assume the claim is finished. That reaction is especially common when a letter blames roof or water damage on \"wear and tear,\" \"old age,\" or a \"long-term leak\". Families often worry that questioning a denial or low offer means going to court or paying legal fees out of pocket. However, pushing back on an incomplete estimate or a disputed denial does not automatically mean a lawsuit.<\/p><p>Sudden damage—such as wind-creased shingles or a burst supply line—frequently gets mislabeled as normal aging during a brief initial walkthrough. Before a dispute ever reaches a courtroom, homeowners have structured options built into their policies and Florida rules. Many policies include an appraisal process specifically for resolving disputes over the dollar amount of a loss, and Florida offers a free mediation program through the Department of Financial Services for many residential property disputes.<\/p><p>Even when the carrier asks for a sworn proof of loss—a formal statement of the amount being claimed—the dispute is still about documenting the facts and the policy language. A licensed public adjuster can inspect the real cause of the damage, prepare the supporting documentation, and negotiate the repair numbers directly with the carrier. If a legal dispute or lawsuit eventually becomes necessary, an insurance attorney handles that stage, while a public adjuster focuses on valuing and negotiating the claim itself.<\/p>", "q": "How do you explain the difference between hiring a public adjuster to negotiate the claim numbers versus hiring an insurance attorney for a legal dispute?"}, {"h": "Who Works for You—and What to Do at Each Layer", "html": "<p>Four layers work for the insurance company, while a public adjuster works strictly for you. In Florida, a public adjuster is licensed by the state and verified through the Department of Financial Services to represent the policyholder. Unlike a contractor—who rebuilds your home but cannot legally negotiate your insurance settlement in Florida without a public adjuster license—a public adjuster reads your policy, documents every room, and handles the back-and-forth with the carrier.<\/p><p>Most public adjusters work on a contingency fee, meaning you pay nothing up front and pay a percentage of the claim settlement. Florida law caps how much a public adjuster can charge, with lower limits after a declared emergency, and requires the fee to be explained in a written contract before you sign. Going through that written agreement line by line before signing keeps every fee and responsibility clear from day one. Whether you hire representation right away or after a low first check, you can take clear steps at every stage of the claim:<\/p><ul><li><b>Before the field adjuster arrives:<\/b> Protect the home from further damage by shutting off water or tarping a roof, take wide and close-up photos first, and keep damaged materials and emergency receipts.<\/li><\/ul><ul><li><b>When the desk adjuster sends an estimate:<\/b> Print the full Statement of Loss, check whether depreciation was held back under an Actual Cash Value calculation, and read the memo line and back of any check before depositing it to make sure it does not say \"full and final settlement\".<\/li><\/ul><ul><li><b>If the file stalls at a supervisor or gets denied:<\/b> Ask for the reason and cited policy section in writing, request the field adjuster's photos and inspection report, and have a licensed public adjuster inspect the property for hidden or sudden damage before tearing anything out.<\/li><\/ul>", "q": "When you sit down with a homeowner before they sign a contract, what do you walk through first so they feel clear on how you work and what your fee covers?"}, {"h": "Your Own Claim File", "html": "<p>Every layer on the insurance company's side builds its decisions from a file of photos, dates, receipts, and written notes. When the carrier's file only holds a ten-minute inspection and a partial repair list, the payout reflects that narrow view. When your own file holds clear photos from day one, emergency receipts, contractor bids, and a complete room-by-room scope, the record tells the full story.<\/p><p>A single missing receipt or unlogged phone call can leave a gap when you compare your records against the carrier's payout sheet. Gathering your declarations page, saving every letter and envelope from the insurer, and checking every line of the repair sheet puts you in command of the facts. If you pull out the paperwork on your kitchen table right now, whose version of your home's damage is written inside that file—yours, or the insurance company's?<\/p>", "q": "What is the single most overlooked piece of paper or photo you wish every homeowner saved in a folder from day one of a loss?"}]}, "3": {"title": "Public Adjuster vs. Insurance Adjuster: Who Works for You on a Homeowners Claim, and Who Doesn't", "lead": "<p>A public adjuster works for you, the policyholder. Every adjuster the insurance company sends, staff or independent, works for the insurance company, however helpful they are.<\/p>", "secs": [{"h": "After the Inspector Leaves", "html": "<p>The truck is gone from your driveway. A person you will never meet is about to read the inspector's notes. That can feel like a door closing. Yet it is closer to a file opening, and a file can take what you add.<\/p><p>A claim can feel like a story you only hear half of. The insurance company puts several people on a file, and each has a different job. Learn those jobs, and you can see exactly where your own paperwork fits.<\/p><p>This guide follows a claim in order. It covers the four layers on the insurance company's side, then the person who can sit on yours. Each step ends with something you can do. It is general information, and every policy is different.<\/p>", "q": "Think of a homeowner who called you right after the carrier's inspector left. What did they say about how it went, and what was the first thing you asked them to show you?"}, {"h": "The Claim Number Is Not the Claim", "html": "<p>The moment you report a loss, the insurance company opens a file and issues a claim number. Four layers of people can touch that file, and you may meet only one. But you do not have to wait in the dark. Write down the claim number and the name of everyone you speak with.<\/p><p>Titles and steps differ from one company to the next. The layers below are a common pattern, not a rulebook. Still, the first moves stay simple: make the house safe, stop more damage, and document what happened.<\/p><p>Gas smell, sparking, water near outlets or a sagging ceiling mean one thing: get to safety first. Then call 911 or your utility company. The photos can wait until the house is safe.<\/p><p>Most policies expect you to take reasonable steps to prevent further damage. Shut off the water at the main shutoff for a burst pipe, set out buckets, move belongings and cover openings. None of these steps needs special training.<\/p><p>Memory fades, and wet drywall goes out with the trash, but photos stay put. Take photos and video before you clean up, from wide shots to close-ups, and include the source of the damage. Keep a cut pipe or a piece of drywall if you can. Save receipts for emergency repairs, supplies, hotel stays and meals.<\/p><p>Hidden moisture adds a clock. Wet drywall, flooring and insulation can grow mold within a few days, so drying out quickly matters. If you see mold, photograph it, disturb it as little as possible, and mention it when the claim is reviewed.<\/p>", "q": "In your area, what do homeowners most often do or skip in the first two days that makes the claim harder later, and what do you wish they had done instead?"}, {"h": "The Field Adjuster: The One You Meet", "html": "<p>The field adjuster is the person who comes to your home to measure, inspect and take photos. Some are employed by the insurance company, and some are independent adjusters hired on contract. Either way, they work for the insurance company, not for you. That is a job description, not an insult, so keep your own record of the damage.<\/p><p>The first phone report can shape the visit. If it mentioned only the roof or one room, the inspection may be set up for that area alone. A first call cannot be redone, but your file can still grow. Write down what you reported and the date, and add every new stain or soft spot you find.<\/p><p>The field adjuster does not decide what you get paid. Water, however, travels along rafters, pipes and subfloors, so damage can turn up rooms away from where it started. A room-by-room walk-through of your own, in daylight, can fill in what a narrow visit leaves out.<\/p><p>A visit that covers one room records one room. The rest of the house is still there, and it can still be documented. Ask the insurance company for the field adjuster's photos and inspection report, then set them beside your own.<\/p>", "q": "Describe a typical field adjuster visit in your market from the homeowner's side: how long it lasts, what usually gets measured, and what usually gets skipped. Give one real example, no names."}, {"h": "The Desk Adjuster: The One You Never Meet", "html": "<p>The desk adjuster is the one you never meet. This person reads the claim notes first and tells the field adjuster what to look at. Later, the same person reviews the final report line by line. Whatever reaches the desk is what the desk can read, so the file you build matters as much as the visit.<\/p><p>A desk cannot read a room it was never shown. Yet you can send your own complete proof of the damage, so nothing gets left off the page. Photos, measurements and a licensed contractor's room-by-room estimate give the desk more to read. You can also ask for a reinspection.<\/p><p>The insurer may also ask for a proof of loss. That is a sworn statement of the amount being claimed. Because it is sworn, read every line before you sign it. If the numbers are unclear, ask someone to review them with you.<\/p><p>Sometimes the answer comes by phone, and a phone call leaves no page behind. Ask the insurer to put the reason in writing, then read the policy section it cites. A written reason gives you something to check line by line.<\/p><p>When a letter cites wear and tear, the cause becomes the question. Sudden damage and long-term wear leave different signs. Wind lifts and creases shingles, and a burst supply line spreads water in a way slow seepage does not. Photograph those signs, and keep the damaged area in place until someone has inspected it again.<\/p>", "q": "Tell about a time the final estimate was missing something the field visit had actually seen, or the reverse. How did you find the gap, and what did you send to close it?"}, {"h": "The Supervisor: The One Who Reviews the Numbers", "html": "<p>Above the desk adjuster sits a supervisor, a manager who watches the claim numbers. A desk adjuster's authority can stop at a certain dollar limit. Past that limit, a file may need someone with more authority to review it. Limits and titles differ by company.<\/p><p>You may never see this layer, but your paper can reach it. A documented, line-by-line estimate that points to your policy language gives a reviewer something concrete to check. A general complaint gives a reviewer far less to work with. The more specific the page, the easier it is to review.<\/p><p>Waiting for a file to move can feel like silence. Keep a log of every call: date, name and what was said. A log turns silence into a timeline you can point to.<\/p><p>Some letters sound final and are not. A reservation of rights letter says the insurer is still reviewing coverage. It is not a denial, yet it is worth having someone review it. Save it with the rest of your paperwork.<\/p>", "q": "When a file stalls waiting on someone higher up, what do you do in the first week, and which one document do you send that tends to get it moving?"}, {"h": "The Legal Department: The Layer in the Background", "html": "<p>The fourth layer is the legal department. Attorneys handle legal disputes and lawsuits, and on a file where nobody disputes the numbers, there is little for them to do. Disagreeing with an estimate is not the same as heading to court.<\/p><p>There are quieter ways to settle a disagreement about the amount. Many policies include an appraisal process. Florida also offers a free mediation program for many residential property disputes through the Department of Financial Services. Check your own policy to see what it says, because every policy is different.<\/p><p>Deadlines can apply to disputes, which is why early steps matter. The practical first step is small. Save the letter and its envelope, and write down the date it arrived. Then check what your policy says about time limits.<\/p>", "q": "What do homeowners most fear when they hear \"dispute\" or \"appraisal,\" and what do you tell them that explains the process without promising any result?"}, {"h": "The Seat on Your Side", "html": "<p>The field adjuster does not work for the homeowner. The desk adjuster does not work for the homeowner. The supervisor does not work for the homeowner. The legal department does not work for the homeowner. That is true of staff adjusters, and it is true of independent adjusters hired on contract. A public adjuster is different. A licensed public adjuster, such as {{FIRST}}, works for the policyholder, not the insurance company.<\/p><p>Policies are written in dense language, and claim paperwork piles up fast. A public adjuster reads the policy, documents the damage, prepares the claim and negotiates with the insurance company for the homeowner. The work usually starts with a conversation about what happened. Then comes an inspection with photos, measurements and moisture readings.<\/p><p>A person can say they work for you, but a license is something you can check. In Florida, public adjusters must be licensed, and you can check a license with the Department of Financial Services. Florida law limits what a public adjuster may charge, and the fee must be explained in a written contract before you sign. Never sign anything you feel pressured to sign on the spot.<\/p><p>A contractor sits in a different seat. A contractor repairs or rebuilds the damage. Under Florida law, a contractor cannot negotiate your insurance settlement unless he or she also holds a public adjuster license. Even so, the two roles can work side by side: one rebuilds while the other handles the policy and the pricing.<\/p><p>Forms and contracts can arrive from several directions. Before you sign anything, find out who the person works for. The answer is one of three: the insurance company, a contractor, or you. Write it next to each name in your folder.<\/p>", "q": "When a homeowner asks why they should bring in someone other than the adjuster who already came out, what do you actually say, the way you would say it at their kitchen table?"}, {"h": "When the First Check Arrives", "html": "<p>An envelope arrives with a check, and the number looks small next to your contractor's quote. A smaller first check can have several causes: depreciation, the deductible, or damage the first inspection did not record. Each of those leaves a trace on paper. Print the full Statement of Loss and estimate, not just the cover letter.<\/p><p>The insurance estimate and a contractor's estimate can differ for plain reasons. Labor rates, materials and the size of the repair area may not match. Ask a licensed local contractor for a room-by-room estimate and set the two lists side by side. Each gap becomes a line you can point to, one at a time.<\/p><p>The repair area can differ too. An estimate may pay to patch one section of a roof or floor. Old materials can break when touched, and new ones may not match across an open room. Ask your contractor to explain in writing why a partial repair will or will not work.<\/p><p>Start with the column labeled depreciation. It shows the amount held back for the age and wear of what was damaged. Some policies pay that held-back amount, called recoverable depreciation, after the repair is done. On those policies, keep every repair invoice.<\/p><p>Two terms explain a lot of first checks. Actual cash value is the repair or replacement cost minus depreciation for age and wear. Replacement cost value is the cost to repair or replace without that subtraction. Your policy shows which one applies to you.<\/p><p>The deductible is the part of the loss you pay before insurance pays. Many Florida policies carry a separate hurricane deductible. It is often a percentage of the home's insured value, such as 2%, 5% or 10%. The declarations page lists it, so you can look it up tonight.<\/p><p>A check and its letter can carry wording that matters. Look for phrases such as \"full and final settlement,\" \"release of all claims\" or \"accord and satisfaction.\" If you see them, pause and have someone review the paperwork before you sign or endorse anything. Take a photo of the check and the letter for your folder.<\/p>", "q": "Tell about the most surprising thing you found on a first-check letter or Statement of Loss: a line, a word, a missing item. What did the homeowner think it meant, and what did it actually mean?"}, {"h": "Your Own File", "html": "<p>Every layer above works from paper, and none of them can see your house the way you can. A folder holds the claim number, names and dates, photos, receipts, letters and the declarations page. Together they put your view of the loss on the record. Start with what is already on your phone and in your mailbox.<\/p><p>Dates are the part of a file nobody else can fill in. Write down when you first noticed each stain, soft spot or swollen baseboard. If it is safe, check the attic, under sinks and inside cabinets, and photograph what you find. Never climb onto the roof; photos from the ground and from inside are enough.<\/p><p>Compare the report with your folder. Photograph anything the report leaves out, such as cracked tiles, warped floors, swollen cabinets or water spots. Each dated photo is one more line the file did not have before.<\/p><p>Hiring help is your decision, and nobody should rush it. A first conversation about what happened is usually free. Before you hire anyone, check the license with the state and read the fee in writing.<\/p><p>Pick up the newest letter from your insurance company and the estimate that came with it. Which room, which line or which word on those pages does not match what you saw with your own eyes?<\/p>", "q": "What is the one item in a homeowner's folder you wish every client showed up with, and what happened on the claim the one time it was missing?"}]}, "4": {"title": "Public Adjuster vs. Insurance Adjuster: Who Works for You on a Homeowners Insurance Claim?", "lead": "<p><b>The short answer:<\/b> The insurance company's staff and independent adjusters work for the insurer. A public adjuster is hired by you, the policyholder, to document your damage, prepare your claim, and negotiate with the insurance company on your behalf.<\/p>", "secs": [{"h": "The stain that kept spreading", "html": "<p>The stain started above the Riveras' living-room window. By the time they noticed it, a yellow line had reached the hallway ceiling.<\/p><p>They reported the damage and received a claim number. That number gave them something to write on every letter, email, and photograph. But it did not tell them who would examine the damage or decide what belonged in the claim.<\/p><p>The Riveras expected someone to inspect the house. They did not realize that several different people could handle the file, often without ever meeting them. The first person they saw would not be the only person reviewing their claim.<\/p><p>That distinction matters. The person who visits the house works on the insurance company's side, not the homeowner's side. The Riveras could ask questions, point out damage, and provide records, but they were not represented by that inspector.<\/p><p>Their first useful step was simple: keep the claim number with the paperwork. They also saved photographs of the stain and the rooms around it. Those records gave them something concrete to refer to when the inspection happened.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, describe an anonymous claim where the homeowner first noticed one visible area of damage, but the important question was what else needed to be documented. What did you look for first?"}, {"h": "The field adjuster arrives", "html": "<p>A field adjuster is usually the person a homeowner meets in person. This is the person who visits the property, looks at the reported damage, takes photographs, and records observations.<\/p><p>Some field adjusters are employees of the insurance company. Others are independent adjusters hired by the company on a contract basis. The word <i>independent<\/i> describes the work arrangement. It does not mean the adjuster represents the homeowner.<\/p><p>The Riveras' field adjuster walked through the living room and looked at the ceiling stain. The visit was brief. The hallway was not included in the estimate they later received.<\/p><p>A short visit does not, by itself, prove that the inspection was wrong. But an area that was never documented can be missing from the claim record. The homeowner should compare the inspection findings with the damage actually present.<\/p><p>The Riveras could make a room-by-room list and photograph each visible area. They could keep wide photographs showing where each room sits in the house, then take close-ups of the damage. If they had receipts for emergency work, those belonged with the claim records too.<\/p><p>There is another important distinction. A field adjuster's inspection is not the same as a complete repair plan. The estimate reflects the information and scope included in the claim review. If the hallway damage is missing, the Riveras can ask how to submit photographs and other documentation for that area.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, tell us about an anonymous inspection where the first visit did not capture the full scope of the damage. What did you notice that changed how you approached the file?"}, {"h": "The desk adjuster they never meet", "html": "<p>After the field visit, the claim may move to a desk adjuster. This person reviews information in the file rather than inspecting the home in person.<\/p><p>A desk adjuster may review photographs, inspection notes, estimates, policy information, and other documents. The desk adjuster may also ask for more information or communicate what the file still needs. The homeowner may never see this person.<\/p><p>That is what happened next for the Riveras. Their paperwork showed the living-room stain, but the hallway was still missing. The desk review could only consider the information that reached the file.<\/p><p>This is where a homeowner's own records become important. A photograph sitting on a phone does not help the desk review unless someone submits it. The Riveras needed to send the hallway photographs and identify the room clearly.<\/p><p>They could also ask for the field inspection report and the line-by-line estimate. Those documents would help them compare what the inspector recorded with what the estimate included. If the documents left out a room, they could point to the specific omission.<\/p><p>A missing item does not automatically mean the insurer must pay for it. The policy and the facts still matter. But a clear record gives the desk adjuster something specific to review instead of a general statement that the estimate is too low.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, what is one thing homeowners in your area often fail to send to the desk adjuster, even though it could help explain the damage? Give an example without identifying a client."}, {"h": "When the file reaches a supervisor", "html": "<p>A supervisor may become involved when a claim needs review beyond the authority of the person handling it. The exact process differs by company and claim.<\/p><p>A desk adjuster may have limits on what they can approve. A question about a larger estimate, a disputed scope, or a decision needing additional review may be referred higher. That does not mean every claim follows the same approval chain.<\/p><p>The Riveras sent photographs of the hallway and asked for the estimate to be reviewed. The file did not change right away. They were left waiting, without knowing whether the added information had been considered.<\/p><p>Silence does not tell a homeowner what is happening inside a claim file. It is better to ask a clear question than to guess. The Riveras could ask whether the new photographs had been received, whether the hallway was included, and whether anything else was needed.<\/p><p>They could keep a simple contact record: the date they sent the documents, who received them, and what response came back. That record would help them explain the history if another person took over the file.<\/p><p>A supervisor's review is still part of the insurance company's process. It does not make the supervisor the homeowner's representative. If the homeowner wants someone working on their side, that is a separate role.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, describe an anonymous claim where a file needed another level of review. What helped move the discussion from a general disagreement to a specific question that could be answered?"}, {"h": "The letter that sounded final", "html": "<p>The next document can feel more final than it really is. A letter may explain that the insurer has made a decision, limited payment, or still needs to review coverage.<\/p><p>The Riveras received a letter that did not include everything they expected. They had to work out whether it was a denial, a partial decision, or a reservation of rights.<\/p><p>A reservation of rights letter means the insurer is still reviewing coverage. It is not the same as a denial. The wording matters, so the Riveras needed to read the whole letter rather than rely on its most alarming sentence.<\/p><p>If the insurer denied part of the claim or made a low offer, the homeowners could ask for the reason in writing. They could also read the policy section the letter cited and compare it with the damage records. A reinspection, added documents, or another estimate may help clarify a disagreement.<\/p><p>Some claim disputes involve the amount of damage or the cost of repairs. A policy may contain an appraisal process for disputes about the amount. Florida also has a Department of Financial Services mediation program for eligible residential property disputes. Mediation is not a court hearing, and it does not decide every kind of dispute.<\/p><p>The insurer may also involve its legal department when a claim raises legal questions or a dispute needs legal review. That does not mean the homeowner is automatically going to court. Disagreeing with an estimate, asking for a reinspection, or submitting more documentation does not by itself mean a lawsuit is coming.<\/p><p>The Riveras could start with the documents in front of them. They could identify what the letter decided, what it left open, and what question they wanted answered. If they did not understand the wording, they could ask a qualified professional to review the letter and policy with them.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, what is a letter or phrase you have seen homeowners misunderstand? Explain what you tell them to look for before they decide what the letter means."}, {"h": "The first check is smaller than the contractor's estimate", "html": "<p>When the first check arrived, the Riveras compared it with the contractor's estimate. The numbers did not match. That difference did not explain itself.<\/p><p>One reason may be depreciation. Actual cash value, or ACV, generally means repair or replacement cost minus depreciation for age and wear. Replacement cost value, or RCV, means the cost to repair or replace without subtracting depreciation.<\/p><p>Some policies pay ACV first and hold back recoverable depreciation until repairs are completed. That can make the first payment smaller than the full repair estimate. The Riveras needed to look at the payment breakdown and policy, not guess from the check amount alone.<\/p><p>Other differences may come from the scope of work. The insurer's estimate may list fewer repairs than the contractor's estimate. The deductible also affects the amount paid. The Riveras could compare the estimates line by line and mark what appeared in one but not the other.<\/p><p>Florida homeowners should also check the declarations page for the hurricane deductible. Florida law generally requires insurers to offer certain hurricane deductible choices, including 2%, 5%, and 10% of the policy dwelling limits, subject to statutory exceptions. The actual dollar amount for the policy's hurricane deductible should appear on the declarations page.<\/p><p>A percentage deductible can be much larger than a flat-dollar deductible. It is not calculated as a percentage of the repair estimate. The Riveras needed to find the actual dollar amount shown in their own policy documents and understand whether it applied to their loss.<\/p><p>The check and its accompanying letter also deserve a careful read. If either uses wording such as \"full and final settlement,\" the Riveras should not guess what signing or endorsing it means. They could ask a qualified professional to review the exact wording before deciding what to do.<\/p><p>Their next step was to gather the insurer's estimate, the contractor's estimate, the declarations page, and the letter that came with the check. That gave someone reviewing the claim a way to compare the numbers and identify the actual points of disagreement.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, what do homeowners most often misunderstand when they compare the first insurance payment with a contractor's estimate? Describe one detail you always tell them to check."}, {"h": "Who works for the homeowner?", "html": "<p>The people handling the insurer's side of the claim have different jobs. The field adjuster inspects. The desk adjuster reviews the file. A supervisor may review matters that need another level of authority. The legal department may become involved when legal review is needed.<\/p><p>None of those roles is the same as a public adjuster hired by the homeowner. A public adjuster is a licensed insurance professional who works for the policyholder, not the insurance company.<\/p><p>A public adjuster can document damage, review the policy, prepare the claim, and negotiate with the insurer on the homeowner's behalf. The work may include inspecting the property, taking photographs and measurements, reviewing letters, and preparing an estimate.<\/p><p>For the Riveras, that would mean having someone examine the missing hallway damage from their side of the claim. It would also mean comparing the insurer's estimate with the contractor's estimate and reviewing the policy language that applies. The goal is to present the claim with supporting information, not promise a particular payment.<\/p><p>In Florida, public adjusters must be licensed by the state. Homeowners can check a license through the Florida Department of Financial Services. A person offering to adjust or negotiate a property claim for compensation should not be treated as a public adjuster simply because they use that title.<\/p><p>A public adjuster's fee should be explained in a written contract before the homeowner signs. Florida law places limits on public adjuster compensation, and the rules can depend on the type of claim and circumstances. The homeowner should read the agreement, understand how the fee is calculated, and ask questions before signing.<\/p><p>A contractor has a different job. Contractors estimate and perform repair work. A contractor's estimate may help document the cost of repairs, but that does not make the contractor the homeowner's public adjuster.<\/p><p>The Riveras did not need to guess which person was on which side. They could check the license, read the contract, and ask exactly what services the professional would provide. The important question was not just who had a title, but who had been hired to represent them.<\/p>", "q": "{{FIRST}}, what do you wish homeowners understood before they decide whether to hire a public adjuster? Give one example of a question they should ask before signing."}, {"h": "Back at the kitchen table", "html": "<p>The Riveras spread the paperwork across the kitchen table. The claim number was on the folder. The photographs showed the living room and hallway. The estimates showed different scopes. The letter explained the insurer's position, but some questions remained.<\/p><p>They could not decide the claim by looking at one number. They needed to understand what had been inspected, what had been included, what the policy said, and what the insurer's letter actually decided.<\/p><p>That is the work a careful claim review begins with. Put the documents together, identify what is missing, and ask specific questions. If the answers depend on the policy or the damage itself, have someone qualified review those materials.<\/p><p>When you look at your own claim file tonight, can you tell who inspected your home, who reviewed the estimate, what the insurer decided, and who is working for you?<\/p><p><b>Florida statute and reference notes<\/b>\\ These are production references for the checked library, not additional homeowner-facing article copy.<\/p><ul><li><b>Public adjuster definition, licensing framework, written contract and compensation limits:<\/b> Florida Statutes §626.854.<\/li><\/ul><ul><li><b>Public adjuster contract disclosures:<\/b> Florida Statutes §626.8796.<\/li><\/ul><ul><li><b>Hurricane deductible options and declarations-page dollar amount:<\/b> Florida Statutes §627.701. The statute includes exceptions and qualifications; the article uses qualified wording.<\/li><\/ul><ul><li><b>Florida Department of Financial Services mediation program:<\/b> Request Mediation &amp; Neutral Evaluation. Eligibility depends on the type and status of the dispute.<\/li><\/ul><ul><li><b>License verification and consumer guidance:<\/b> Florida DFS Licensee Search.<\/li><\/ul>", "q": ""}]}};
var NAMES={'1':'Thorough','2':'Straight to the point','3':'Step by step','4':'Story-led'};
var CSS='.cta-art .vt{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;margin:10px 0 4px}.cta-art .vt button{font:700 12.5px Inter,sans-serif;padding:7px 12px;border-radius:999px;border:1px solid #D9D6CE;background:#fff;color:#333;cursor:pointer}.cta-art .vt button[aria-pressed=true]{background:#111;color:#F5A524;border-color:#111}.cta-art .vh{font-size:12px;color:#777;margin:2px 0 12px}.cta-art .vb{display:none}.cta-art .vb.on{display:block}.cta-art .vb h2{margin-top:6px}.cta-art .vb h3{font-size:1.18em;margin:26px 0 8px}.cta-art .vb p{line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 14px}.cta-art .vb ul,.cta-art .vb ol{margin:0 0 14px 22px;line-height:1.7}.cta-art .vb li{margin:4px 0}.cta-art .vl{font-size:1.08em}';
function esc(x){return String(x).replace(/[&<>"]/g,function(c){return {'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;'}[c]})}
function E(x,o){return o.raw?String(x):esc(x)}
function T(h,o){return h.split('{{FIRST}}').join(E(o.fn,o))}
window.CTArticle={
 html:function(o){
  var k=Object.keys(V);
  var tabs='<div class="vt" role="tablist">'+k.map(function(n,i){return '<button type="button" data-v="'+n+'" aria-pressed="'+(i===0)+'">V'+n+' &middot; '+NAMES[n]+'</button>'}).join('')+'</div><p class="vh">Same facts, four voices. The adjuster picks one, and it becomes his.</p>';
  var bodies=k.map(function(n,i){var x=V[n];return '<div class="vb'+(i===0?' on':'')+'" data-vb="'+n+'"><h2>'+T(x.title,o)+'</h2><p class="by">'+E(o.place,o)+' &middot; Approved by '+E(o.name,o)+', Licensed Public Adjuster</p><div class="vl">'+T(x.lead,o)+'</div>'+x.secs.map(function(s){return '<h3>'+T(s.h,o)+'</h3>'+T(s.html,o)}).join('')+'</div>'}).join('');
  return '<div class="sec team cta-art"><p class="tk" style="color:#0E9F6E">PILLAR ARTICLE &middot; HOMEOWNER GUIDE</p>'+tabs+bodies+(o.after||'')+'</div>';
 },
 bind:function(root){
  if(!document.getElementById('cta-art-css')){var st=document.createElement('style');st.id='cta-art-css';st.textContent=CSS;document.head.appendChild(st)}
  (root||document).addEventListener('click',function(e){var b=e.target.closest&&e.target.closest('.cta-art .vt button');if(!b)return;var a=b.closest('.cta-art');
   a.querySelectorAll('.vt button').forEach(function(x){x.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(x===b))});
   a.querySelectorAll('.vb').forEach(function(x){x.classList.toggle('on',x.getAttribute('data-vb')===b.getAttribute('data-v'))});
   var t=a.querySelector('.vt').getBoundingClientRect().top;if(t<0)window.scrollBy({top:t-80,behavior:'smooth'})});
 }
};
})();
